{% trace-scenario title="Why did the agent pick that tool?" prompt="A multi-step research agent built on your team's chosen framework just returned a wrong answer. You need to know exactly which tool call it made, what state it was carrying at that branch, and why the framework chose that path over the obvious alternative — before you can decide whether this is a prompt problem, a tool-selection problem, or a framework bug." outcome="Latitude is built to answer that specific question with framework-native depth. Uncoil is built to answer the next one: is this a one-off, or a regression that should block the next deploy, and what did it cost you either way?" /%}

## At a glance

| Dimension | Latitude | Uncoil |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Core strength** | Deep, framework-native tracing of agent decision paths — tool calls, state transitions, branch logic | Integrated tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, and experiments in one pipeline |
| **Primary use case** | Development-time debugging inside your agent framework | Development *and* production: quality gates, cost governance, regression detection |
| **Evaluation** | Not the product's center of gravity; typically wired to external scripts or judges | Native test case library, batch runner, LLM-as-judge, versioned test suites |
| **Cost tracking** | Token counts are visible; per-call pricing is usually your own integration | Automatic, real-time, per-call cost by model and provider |
| **Session/state tracking** | Framework-native state and decision-tree visibility, tied to how your framework represents an agent run | Native multi-turn session model, independent of which framework (or no framework) you use |
| **Framework coupling** | Deepest value when you're already committed to the framework it understands well | Framework-agnostic; instruments the call, not the orchestration layer |
| **Best for** | Engineers debugging *why* an agent decided what it decided, mid-development | Teams that need the same pipeline to answer "is this good enough to ship" and "what does it cost" |

### The operating model

**Latitude** answers *what did the agent do, and why, inside this framework's execution model*. **Uncoil** answers *is what the agent did good enough, and at what cost, everywhere it runs*. Neither question is optional in a mature agent product — the difference is which one each platform treats as its center of gravity, and how far you have to reach outside the tool to answer the other.

## Opening scenario: the wrong-answer ticket

Your team ships a research agent that chains a search tool, a summarizer, and a citation checker. It's built on an agent orchestration framework — a graph-based executor, a crew-of-agents pattern, or something closer to hand-rolled state machine, it doesn't much matter which. A support ticket comes in: the agent cited a source that doesn't say what the agent claimed it says. Four different people now want four different things from the same incident.

**The engineer who built the agent** wants to see the exact execution path: which node in the graph ran, what the tool call's arguments were, what the intermediate state looked like right before the citation step, and whether a conditional branch fired the way it was supposed to.

**The team lead** wants to know whether this is a one-off hallucination or a pattern. Has this happened on other runs with a similar shape? Is there a test case that should have caught it?

**The person who owns the budget** wants to know what this agent costs per resolved ticket, whether the citation-checker step is worth its latency and token cost, and whether a cheaper model would have caught the same error.

**The engineering manager** wants an answer to "do we ship the next version," not a trace file. They want a quality gate, not a debugging session.

These aren't competing priorities — they're sequential. First you need to see what happened at the framework level (Latitude's strength). Then you need to decide, systematically and repeatably, whether it's acceptable and what it costs (Uncoil's strength). Most teams that reach for only one of these tools eventually build, borrow, or bolt on the other half.

## Core difference: decision-path depth vs. integrated validation

{% highlight %}The choice between Latitude and Uncoil is not about which one traces better. It is about whether your bottleneck is understanding a single agent's internal decision path inside your framework, or turning many traces into a repeatable answer about quality and cost.{% /highlight %}

**Latitude** is built for *engineers who live inside a specific agent framework and need to see exactly how it thinks*. Its value concentrates at one stage: showing the step-by-step decision tree an agent produced — which branch a graph took, which tool a planner selected, how state mutated between steps — rendered in a way that maps cleanly onto the framework's own execution model. This is the layer that makes "why did it do that" answerable in minutes instead of hours of log-reading. The trade-off: evaluation, cost accounting, and turning many traces into a release decision are typically left to you, wired up separately.

**Uncoil** is built for *teams that need the same evidence to answer both "what happened" and "is it good enough to ship, at what cost"*. Tracing, a native evaluation framework, automatic per-call cost tracking, and an experiment framework sit in one product, framework-agnostic, so the same pipeline that helps you debug in development is the one that gates production releases and tracks spend. The trade-off: Uncoil does not try to be the deepest possible window into one specific framework's internal execution semantics — it instruments the call boundary consistently across frameworks, which is a different, shallower kind of depth at the framework layer, in exchange for consistency across your whole system and across environments.

Neither trade-off is a defect. They're built for different moments in the same agent's lifecycle. For broader context on what "observability" should mean for agent systems generally, our [complete guide to AI agent observability](/blog/ai-agent-observability-complete-guide) covers the full signal set — traces, evaluations, cost, and the operating practices that connect them — which is useful background before deciding how much of that signal set you want from one vendor versus assembled yourself.

## The architecture comparison

![Diagram comparing Latitude's framework-native tracing pipeline against Uncoil's single integrated pipeline for tracing, evaluation, cost, and experiments](/media/latitude-vs-uncoil-hero.svg)

### Latitude: depth concentrated at the decision-path layer

Latitude's architecture is organized around making one thing legible: what did the agent's execution look like, step by step, inside the framework that built it. In practice that means:

- **Framework-native instrumentation.** Traces are structured to mirror how your framework represents an agent run — nodes, edges, planner decisions, tool invocations, and state — rather than a generic flat span list. If your framework has a graph, you see a graph; if it has a plan-and-execute loop, you see the plan and the execution against it.
- **Step-level inspection.** You can drill into any single decision point: what input reached it, what the model returned, which branch fired, and what changed in state afterward. This is the debugging workflow Latitude optimizes for.
- **Development-first workflow.** The tooling assumes an engineer is looking at one run (or a handful) trying to understand a specific behavior, not scanning thousands of runs for a statistical pattern.
- **Evaluation and cost as adjacent concerns.** You can typically export or query trace data and wire it into an external evaluation harness or a cost script, but this is glue you build and maintain, not a first-class part of the product's workflow.

The result: when the question is "why did this one run do that," Latitude gets you to an answer fast, in the vocabulary your framework already uses. When the question is "across the last 10,000 runs, is quality trending down, and what's it costing us," you're mostly on your own.

### Uncoil: one pipeline, four jobs

Uncoil's architecture optimizes for a different property: the same evidence should support debugging, evaluation, cost governance, and experimentation, in development and in production, without re-instrumenting or re-platforming between them.

- **Framework-agnostic tracing.** Spans and sessions are captured at the call boundary — the LLM call, the tool call, the retrieval call — so instrumentation works the same whether you're using a graph framework, a lighter orchestration library, or no framework at all.
- **Native evaluation.** Test cases live in the platform: exact match, substring, semantic similarity, and LLM-as-judge evaluators, versioned and re-runnable against historical or live traces, without external scripts.
- **Automatic cost tracking.** Every LLM call is costed against current provider pricing automatically; no manual pricing sheet to maintain as providers change rates.
- **Native session and state tracking.** Multi-turn interactions roll up as a session, independent of any single framework's internal state representation, so cost and quality can be measured per conversation, not just per call.
- **Experiment framework.** Prompt versions, model choices, and routing strategies can be compared with the same evaluation criteria, with statistical confidence built in rather than assembled from spreadsheet exports.

The result: Uncoil reaches "is this good enough to ship, and what does it cost" quickly, because that's the question the pipeline is built to answer at every stage — but it does not attempt to be a framework-specific decision-tree debugger the way Latitude is. If your hardest problem is understanding one agent's internal reasoning path in detail, Uncoil's traces are useful but comparatively general-purpose.

## Scenario 1: debugging a multi-tool agent during development

Your team is three weeks into building an agent that plans a multi-step task, selects from eight tools, and occasionally needs to backtrack when a tool call fails or returns something unusable. You're seeing intermittent bad outcomes and need to understand the decision path, not just the final output.

### With Latitude

You open the trace for a failing run and see it rendered in the shape of your framework's execution graph: the planning node, the branch it selected, the tool call with its exact arguments, the tool's raw response, and the state mutation that followed. You can step through the run node by node. Within minutes you spot it: the planner chose a fallback tool because a condition on the primary tool's availability evaluated incorrectly under a specific input shape — a framework-level bug, not a prompting problem.

```text
// Illustrative: the kind of step-level detail Latitude surfaces
Node: plan_next_action
  input.state.available_tools: ["search", "summarize", "cite_check"]
  input.state.last_error: TimeoutError("search", 4200ms)
  decision: fallback_to_secondary_search
  reasoning_trace: "primary search tool marked unavailable after timeout;
                    condition `retries < 2` evaluated true but branch
                    routed to fallback before retry counter incremented"
  → state.retries stayed at 0 across three consecutive fallbacks
```

This is the workflow Latitude is built for: a specific, reproducible bug in decision logic, found by reading the framework's own execution semantics rather than reconstructing them from generic spans.

### With Uncoil

The same trace shows you the tool call, its input and output, and the surrounding span tree — enough to see that the search tool timed out and a fallback fired repeatedly. What Uncoil adds that Latitude doesn't concentrate on: this pattern shows up as a *repeatable test case*. You turn the failing input into a regression test, add it to a suite, and now every future change to the planning logic gets checked against it automatically, with pass/fail history over time — not just visibility into this one run.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Two different debugging instincts" title="Framework depth finds the bug faster; integrated evaluation keeps it fixed." body="Latitude's step-level view is genuinely the faster path to spotting a framework-level logic bug like a stuck retry counter, because the trace is shaped like your framework's own execution model. Uncoil's advantage shows up after the bug is found: turning it into a versioned regression test means the same failure mode gets caught automatically before it reaches production again, without a human re-reading traces. Mature teams do both — debug with framework-native depth, then encode what they learned as an Uncoil test case." tone="yellow" /%}

## Scenario 2: production with cost and quality constraints

Your research agent is live. You're processing roughly 50,000 requests a month, product wants cost per resolved request under a target, and the incident above means engineering leadership wants an automated quality gate before every deploy — not a promise that someone will eyeball a sample of outputs.

![Illustrative monthly cost comparison at 50,000 requests per month showing Latitude paired with a manually built evaluation stack against Uncoil's integrated pipeline](/media/latitude-vs-uncoil-cost-breakdown.svg)

### With Latitude

Latitude continues to be useful whenever a production incident needs the same kind of decision-path debugging as the development scenario above — that value doesn't go away in production. But the two new requirements, automated quality gating and per-call cost governance, are not what the product concentrates on. In practice, a team in this position typically:

- Builds an evaluation runner that pulls traces via the API and scores them against internally maintained test criteria (LLM-as-judge or rule-based), because there's no native test case library to reuse.
- Maintains a pricing integration to convert token counts into dollars, and updates it when provider pricing changes.
- Builds its own regression-alerting logic — something that says "quality metric X dropped below threshold Y, block the deploy" — because that isn't a first-class product feature.

**Illustrative monthly cost at 50,000 requests/month:** platform cost around **$450**, plus roughly a quarter of one engineer's time (~$3,000/month at a loaded engineering rate) maintaining the evaluation harness and judge prompts, plus a modest ongoing cost (~$350/month) keeping the cost-calculation glue code correct as pricing and model mixes change. That's approximately **$3,800/month, or $45,600/year** — dominated by engineering time, not platform fees. Numbers here are a worked illustration, not a vendor price list; your actual mix of engineering time and platform spend will vary with team size and how much of this you've already built.

### With Uncoil

The same 50,000 monthly requests flow through tracing, evaluation, and cost tracking as one pipeline. Test cases encoding the quality bar ("citation must be traceable to a retrieved source," "response must not exceed 3 tool calls") live in the platform, versioned, and run automatically against new traces. Cost per request, per session, and per model is visible without a separate pricing integration. A regression alert — "citation-accuracy score dropped 8 points against the last release" — is a threshold you configure once, not a script you maintain.

**Illustrative monthly cost at 50,000 requests/month:** platform cost around **$950** for the integrated tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, and experiment pipeline, plus a light maintenance load (~$600/month, roughly 0.05 FTE) for SDK updates and refining test cases as the product changes. That's approximately **$1,550/month, or $18,600/year** — a bit under half of the Latitude-plus-external-stack estimate above, mostly because evaluation and cost tracking don't require separately built and maintained tooling.

> **Read the comparison honestly:** the gap here is not "Latitude is expensive." Latitude's own platform cost in this illustration is *lower* than Uncoil's. The gap is the engineering time required to build the other half of production readiness — evaluation and cost governance — versus getting it as part of the product. If your team already has that harness built and stable, the delta shrinks substantially.

## Scenario 3: multi-agent systems

You're building a system with several specialized agents — a planner, a retriever, a writer, and a reviewer — coordinating on a shared task, possibly across more than one underlying framework as the system grows.

### With Latitude

Multi-agent coordination is where framework-native tracing earns its keep most clearly. Understanding *why* the planner handed a subtask to the writer instead of the reviewer, or why two agents disagreed on the same sub-result, requires seeing each agent's internal decision path and how they intersect — exactly the step-level, framework-shaped view Latitude specializes in. If your multi-agent framework has a defined message-passing or handoff protocol, Latitude's trace view tends to mirror that protocol directly, which is a meaningfully faster read than a flat, generic span list when you're debugging a coordination failure.

### With Uncoil

Cross-agent coordination failures are visible in Uncoil too, at the span and session level — you can see each agent's calls, their sequencing, and where handoffs happened. What Uncoil adds is the ability to evaluate the *system's* output, not just an individual agent's step: a test case can assert on the final reviewed output regardless of which internal path produced it, and cost rolls up across the whole multi-agent session, so you can see what a single completed task cost end-to-end, not just what one agent's calls cost. If your multi-agent system spans agents built on different underlying frameworks — increasingly common as systems grow — Uncoil's framework-agnostic instrumentation avoids needing a separate framework-specific tracer per component.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Coordination debugging" title="Depth per agent vs. accountability for the system." body="Latitude's framework-native view is the better tool for the question 'why did agent A hand off to agent B instead of C, and was that handoff logic correct.' Uncoil's integrated evaluation and session rollup is the better tool for the question 'did the multi-agent system, as a whole, produce an acceptable and affordable result.' Complex multi-agent products tend to need both questions answered, often in the same incident review." tone="dark" /%}

## Deep comparison: feature by feature

### Tracing depth and instrumentation

**Latitude:**
- Traces are shaped around your framework's own execution model: nodes, branches, planner decisions, and state transitions rendered close to how the framework represents them internally.
- Step-level inspection is built for a human reading one run closely, not for scanning thousands of runs programmatically.
- Depth is strongest where the framework integration is deepest; coverage and fidelity for less common or custom orchestration patterns will vary, so verify current framework support directly with Latitude before committing.

**Uncoil:**
- Traces are captured at the call boundary (LLM call, tool call, retrieval call) consistently, regardless of framework, plus a native multi-turn session model layered on top.
- Built for both close reading of a single run and for aggregate analysis across large volumes of traces — filtering, grouping, and trend detection.
- Framework-agnostic by design, which trades some framework-specific vocabulary for consistency across a system that may use more than one framework, or none.

**Verdict:** Latitude wins on framework-native decision-path depth for a single run. Uncoil wins on consistency and aggregate analysis across many runs and across frameworks. Neither is "more detailed" in the abstract — they're detailed about different things.

### Evaluation

**Latitude:**
- Evaluation is not the product's center of gravity. Expect to pull trace data via API and score it with your own criteria, judge prompts, and storage.
- No native, versioned test case library as a first-class workflow; you build and maintain this yourself, or via an external tool.
- Well suited to a team that already has, or wants full control over, its own evaluation pipeline and judge logic.

**Uncoil:**
- Native test case library: define expected behavior, version it, and re-run it against historical or live traces.
- Built-in evaluators: exact match, substring, semantic similarity, and LLM-as-judge, without external scripting.
- Regression detection and threshold alerts are configuration, not code you write and maintain.

**Verdict:** Uncoil wins decisively if evaluation velocity matters to you — going from "we noticed a bad output" to "we have a test that will catch it automatically" is a config change, not a build project. Latitude is adequate only if you're comfortable building and owning that layer yourself, which some teams with unusual evaluation criteria genuinely prefer.

### Cost tracking

**Latitude:**
- Token counts are visible in traces, as they are on essentially every tracing tool.
- Per-call dollar cost typically requires your own pricing integration, kept current as providers change rates and you add models.
- No built-in cost trending, per-session rollups, or cost-threshold alerting as native features.

**Uncoil:**
- Automatic, real-time cost calculation per call, matched to current provider pricing.
- Cost rolls up by model, by session, by agent, with trending over time.
- Threshold alerts ("average cost per session exceeded $X") are configuration.

**Verdict:** Uncoil wins clearly here. Cost governance is close to a checkbox in Uncoil and a standing maintenance task in Latitude's model.

### Session and state tracking

**Latitude:**
- State is tracked the way your framework represents it — which is exactly what makes step-level debugging so legible, but ties the representation to that framework's internal model.
- Cross-framework or cross-agent session views are not the product's focus; expect to reconcile state representations yourself if your system spans more than one framework.

**Uncoil:**
- Native, framework-independent session model: a multi-turn interaction is a first-class object with its own cost and quality rollups.
- Works the same whether the underlying agent uses one framework, several, or none.

**Verdict:** Uncoil wins on consistency and system-level session tracking. Latitude wins on fidelity to a single framework's own state model, which matters most when you're debugging inside that framework specifically.

## Cost analysis: illustrative 12-month view at 50,000 requests/month

The table below extends the scenario-2 numbers into an annual view, assuming a small team (roughly 8-12 engineers), 50,000 requests/month, and a product that needs both development-time debugging and production quality gates within the first quarter.

### Latitude, paired with a self-built evaluation and cost layer

| Category | Item | Monthly | Annual |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Platform | Framework-native tracing | $450 | $5,400 |
| Evaluation build | Initial evaluation harness (one-time, amortized) + judge prompt maintenance | $3,000 | $36,000 |
| Cost-tracking glue | Pricing integration, kept current as providers change rates | $350 | $4,200 |
| **Total** | | **$3,800** | **$45,600** |

### Uncoil, integrated

| Category | Item | Monthly | Annual |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Platform | Tracing + evaluation + cost tracking + experiments | $950 | $11,400 |
| Setup | Account, SDK integration, first test case (few days, one-time, amortized) | ~$50 | ~$600 |
| Maintenance | SDK updates, refining test cases (~0.05 FTE) | $600 | $7,200 |
| **Total** | | **$1,550** | **$18,600** |

{% highlight %}In this illustration, Uncoil's platform fee alone is roughly double Latitude's — but the total cost of ownership favors Uncoil by about 2.5x, because evaluation and cost governance are not a separate build project. The comparison changes meaningfully if your team already has a stable, well-maintained evaluation harness: at that point, the delta shrinks to close to Uncoil's platform premium alone.{% /highlight %}

> **These are illustrative figures, not a quote.** Actual platform pricing, team composition, and how much evaluation tooling you've already built will change both totals. Treat the shape of the comparison — platform-fee-plus-engineering-time versus a higher platform fee with less engineering time — as the takeaway, not the specific dollar figures.

## Trade-offs and honest boundaries

### Choose Latitude when:

**1. Framework-native decision-path debugging is your actual bottleneck.**
If your team spends real engineering hours each week trying to understand *why* your specific framework routed an agent down one branch instead of another, Latitude's step-level, framework-shaped view is genuinely the faster tool for that job. This is not a claim Uncoil's more general call-boundary tracing can fully match.

**2. You already have a mature, working evaluation and cost pipeline.**
If your team has already built and maintained solid evaluation scripts and cost-calculation tooling, the "Uncoil saves you a build" argument weakens substantially. In that case Latitude's deeper framework tracing may be worth adding without giving up what you've already built.

**3. Your agent's complexity lives almost entirely at the framework/orchestration layer.**
Some agents are simple prompts with a straightforward call pattern; others are dense graphs with many conditional branches and heavy internal state. The more complexity lives in the latter, the more Latitude's framework-native view pays for itself.

**4. You need to audit or extend the framework's own execution logic, not just the model's outputs.**
When the bug is in your orchestration code — a race condition, a stuck retry counter, a misrouted branch — a tool that speaks your framework's execution vocabulary finds it faster than one that treats every framework as a generic span tree.

### Choose Uncoil when:

**1. You need the same evidence to support development *and* production decisions.**
Building one evaluation and cost pipeline that works from your first prototype through your production release, without a re-platforming step in between, is Uncoil's central value proposition.

**2. Cost governance is a stated business requirement, not an engineering nice-to-have.**
If finance or product leadership expects per-request cost visibility and trending without a custom pricing integration, Uncoil's automatic cost tracking removes a recurring maintenance task, not just a one-time build.

**3. Your system spans more than one framework, or none.**
Multi-agent systems increasingly mix frameworks, or use no formal orchestration framework at all for simpler agents. Uncoil's call-boundary instrumentation is consistent regardless, where a framework-native tool's value is concentrated on the framework(s) it understands deeply.

**4. Evaluation velocity — turning an observed failure into an automated regression test — is your primary lever for shipping confidently.**
If the real friction in your process is "we saw a bad output once and have no reliable way to make sure it doesn't happen again," Uncoil's native test case library addresses that directly.

### A note on our earlier evaluation guide

If you haven't yet defined what "good enough to ship" means for your agent in measurable terms, that's a prerequisite for either platform's evaluation layer paying off. Our [practical guide to AI agent evaluation](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) walks through building representative test datasets and connecting evaluation results to actual release decisions — worth reading regardless of which tracing tool you choose, since a well-designed test suite is portable between them.

### Hybrid approach: framework-native tracing plus integrated evaluation

Some teams genuinely need both and run them together rather than picking one:

- **Upside:** Latitude's framework-native depth for development-time debugging, Uncoil's integrated evaluation and cost tracking for production gates — each doing the part it's strongest at.
- **Downside:** two integrations to maintain, and you'll need to reconcile trace identifiers or timestamps if you want to correlate a Latitude debugging session with an Uncoil test-case failure on the same underlying run.
- **When it's worth it:** teams with genuinely complex framework-level logic (dense multi-agent graphs, heavy conditional branching) *and* a hard production quality-gate requirement, where neither tool's core strength is optional.

> **Don't run both "just in case."** Two tracing integrations add real overhead — SDK maintenance, two dashboards to check, two places incident data can live. Add the second platform when a concrete need (framework-depth debugging, or production quality gates) is unmet by the first, not preemptively.

## Decision framework for practitioners and leaders

![Quadrant chart plotting Latitude, Uncoil, manual tooling, and a hybrid approach against framework-level debugging depth and production quality governance](/media/latitude-vs-uncoil-decision-framework.svg)

### For technical practitioners

| Question | Score if yes | Suggests |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Do you spend real weekly time debugging framework-level decision logic (branches, retries, state)? | +5 Latitude | Framework-native depth pays off directly |
| Is your team already using a single, well-understood orchestration framework? | +3 Latitude | Framework-specific tracing has less coverage risk |
| Do you need an automated quality gate before every production deploy? | +5 Uncoil | Native evaluation removes a build project |
| Is cost-per-request visibility a stated requirement from product or finance? | +4 Uncoil | Automatic cost tracking avoids a maintenance burden |
| Does your system span more than one framework, or agents with no formal framework? | +3 Uncoil | Framework-agnostic instrumentation avoids fragmentation |
| Do you already have a stable, maintained evaluation and cost pipeline? | +3 Latitude | The "Uncoil saves a build" argument weakens |
| Is most of your agent's complexity in orchestration logic rather than prompt/output quality? | +3 Latitude | Decision-path depth matters more than output evaluation |

**Scoring:** Latitude score above 12 suggests framework-native tracing is worth adopting or keeping as your primary tool. Uncoil score above 12 suggests integrated evaluation and cost tracking is your bottleneck. Both scores high generally points to the hybrid approach above, not a single-tool decision.

### For engineering and product leaders

**What's actually slowing you down right now?**
- **"We can't figure out why the agent does what it does"** → Latitude's framework-native depth addresses this directly.
- **"We can't tell if a change made things better or worse before it ships"** → Uncoil's native evaluation and experiment framework addresses this directly.
- **"We don't know what this is costing us"** → Uncoil's automatic cost tracking addresses this directly; Latitude requires you to build it.

**Team size and existing tooling:**
- **Small team, no existing evaluation harness:** Uncoil's integrated pipeline avoids a build project you likely can't staff separately.
- **Team with a mature internal evaluation stack already:** Latitude's framework-native tracing can be a strong addition without displacing what you've built.
- **Team building on multiple frameworks, or planning to:** Uncoil's framework-agnostic model avoids re-instrumenting per framework.

**Compliance and audit posture:**
Neither platform's exact data handling, retention, or deployment options should be assumed from this article — verify current data residency, retention, and security posture directly with each vendor against your specific compliance requirements before committing either as a system of record for production incident data.

## Migration paths and hybrid use

### Adding Uncoil alongside existing Latitude instrumentation (low-risk)

1. **Parallel instrumentation (weeks 1-2):** add the Uncoil SDK alongside your existing Latitude instrumentation; both can typically receive the same underlying call data without conflicting.
2. **Build your first test cases (days, not weeks):** encode two or three known failure modes — including ones you originally found via Latitude's step-level debugging — as Uncoil test cases.
3. **Run both for a month:** use Latitude when a new bug needs framework-level debugging; use Uncoil's regression suite to confirm the fix holds over time and doesn't cost more.
4. **Decide deliberately:** keep both if each is earning its keep, or drop the one that isn't after you have real usage data on which questions you're actually asking week to week.

### Adding Latitude alongside existing Uncoil instrumentation

1. **Identify the actual friction:** confirm that framework-level decision-path debugging, specifically, is the unmet need — not a symptom that better Uncoil test cases or panel review would also fix.
2. **Instrument narrowly first:** add Latitude to the specific agent or framework component where debugging friction is highest, rather than your whole system, to validate the value before expanding.
3. **Keep evaluation and cost tracking on Uncoil:** there's rarely a reason to duplicate evaluation logic once it exists; use Latitude purely for the decision-path visibility it adds.

**Migration risk in both directions is low**, because these tools solve adjacent, not overlapping, problems — you are typically adding coverage, not replacing a system of record.

> **Trade-off:** Latitude reduces workflow-building friction before launch, while Uncoil earns its value after real traffic creates production failures and cost questions.

## Action checklist

- [ ] **Name the actual bottleneck.** Is it "we can't see why the agent decided that" (Latitude), or "we can't systematically tell if a change is safe to ship, and what it costs" (Uncoil)?
- [ ] **Audit what you've already built.** If you have a working evaluation harness and pricing integration, Latitude's incremental value rises and Uncoil's build-avoidance argument shrinks.
- [ ] **Map your framework footprint.** Single, well-supported framework favors Latitude's depth; multiple frameworks or framework-free agents favor Uncoil's consistency.
- [ ] **Estimate your real TCO,** not just platform fees — include the engineering time either path requires, using the worked table above as a starting structure, not a quote.
- [ ] **Run a short parallel pilot** if undecided: a few weeks of both, instrumented on the same agent, with each tool used only for the question it's built to answer.
- [ ] **Set a review date.** Reassess after 30 days with real usage data: which tool did you actually open when something went wrong, and which one told you whether it was safe to ship?

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Does Latitude replace the need for a native evaluation framework?" answer="Not by design. Latitude's strength is framework-native decision-path tracing for development-time debugging; evaluation is typically something you wire up externally rather than a first-class, versioned test case library built into the product. If automated, repeatable quality gates before every deploy are a requirement, most teams either build that layer themselves on top of Latitude's traces or pair it with a platform like Uncoil that treats evaluation as core." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can I use Latitude and Uncoil together?" answer="Yes. They solve adjacent, mostly non-overlapping problems — Latitude for framework-native decision-path debugging, Uncoil for integrated evaluation, cost tracking, and experiments — so running both is a low-risk way to get depth in development and governance in production. The main overhead is maintaining two SDKs and, if you want to correlate incidents across both, reconciling trace identifiers or timestamps between them." /%}

{% faq-item question="Does Latitude track cost the way Uncoil does?" answer="Token counts are typically visible in Latitude's traces, since that's provided by the model APIs themselves, but converting that into per-call dollar cost, trending it over time, and rolling it up by session generally requires your own pricing integration and dashboarding. Uncoil calculates cost automatically against current provider pricing as a native feature. If cost governance is a stated business requirement, verify Latitude's current cost-tracking capabilities directly, since product features change, but plan for this to require engineering time either way if it's not natively covered." /%}

{% faq-item question="What's the biggest limitation of choosing Uncoil over Latitude?" answer="Uncoil's tracing is framework-agnostic by design, instrumented at the call boundary rather than mirroring any single framework's internal execution model. That consistency is valuable across a system with multiple frameworks, but it means Uncoil's traces are comparatively general-purpose next to a tool built specifically to render one framework's decision tree, branches, and state transitions in that framework's own vocabulary. If your hardest debugging problems live deep inside your orchestration framework's logic, a framework-native tracer will often get you to the answer faster." /%}

{% faq-item question="How do I decide without spending weeks evaluating both platforms?" answer="Look at your last five debugging or shipping incidents and ask which question was actually blocking you each time: 'why did the agent do that' (Latitude's question) or 'is this safe to ship, and what does it cost' (Uncoil's question). If it was consistently the first, prioritize framework-native tracing. If it was consistently the second, prioritize integrated evaluation and cost tracking. If it was a mix, the honest answer is you likely need both eventually — start with whichever question cost you more engineering time in the last quarter, and treat the other as a near-term addition rather than a competing final choice." /%}
